WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.088
Federated averaging trains one model for many clients.

2
00:00:03.692 --> 00:00:07.570
When their data differ, the average can leave some of them behind.

3
00:00:07.203 --> 00:00:16.630
On CIFAR-10 with strongly skewed data, FedAvg averages forty-eight percent, while its worst tenth of clients reaches barely nineteen.

4
00:00:17.433 --> 00:00:23.192
FedPCD keeps the average as the primary direction, and turns every client into a constraint:

5
00:00:23.888 --> 00:00:26.535
each must make a minimum of progress.

6
00:00:26.027 --> 00:00:29.580
When the average already satisfies everyone, nothing changes.

7
00:00:30.183 --> 00:00:36.801
Otherwise it bends, just enough, and a short memory keeps protecting clients that sit out a round.

8
00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:45.371
In the same setting, FedPCD reaches seventy-one point nine percent, and its worst ten percent of clients reach fifty-two point nine.

9
00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:49.338
It has the highest global accuracy in all eighteen settings tested.

10
00:00:49.303 --> 00:00:52.600
The average should lead, but not at any client's expense.
